    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis examines the phenomenon of \"political fandoms\" and their impact on democratic backsliding in South Korea. Political fandoms are groups of emotionally devoted supporters of specific politicians or parties, primarily organized through online platforms. The thesis argues that these fandoms have fueled affective polarization and undermined democratic norms in South Korea under the recent administrations of Moon Jae-in (2017-2022) and Yoon Suk-yeol (2022-present).\r\nDrawing on comparative politics literature, the thesis identifies patterns of democratic backsliding globally and highlights the role of affective polarization in enabling these trends. It analyzes the emergence of political fandoms in South Korea, characterized by intense emotional bonds, an us-vs-them mentality, and the fusion of partisan identity with personal and moral worth. Through coordinated digital activism and the spread of disinformation, fandoms have amplified polarization and transformed political discourse.\r\nThe thesis conducts an empirical analysis of tweets by fandom and non-fandom accounts, finding that fandom accounts use significantly more polarizing and violent rhetoric. It situates the Korean case within the global context of affectively polarized politics and considers the historical roots and potential limits of fandom influence.\r\nTo address the challenges posed by fandoms to democracy, the thesis argues for a multifaceted approach targeting both the supply and demand sides of fandom politics, including regulating digital platforms, strengthening institutional checks, and investing in civic education. Ultimately, the future of South Korean democracy depends on citizens' ability to reject divisive politics and build a more inclusive, pluralistic political culture. The thesis contends that this challenge also presents an opportunity for democratic renewal and resilience.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
